  "answer_text": "Great question! It can feel a little awkward at first, but with a few tips it becomes much easier. Here's how to handle eye contact with someone who has a lazy eye (also called strabismus):\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Understand What's Actually Happening**\nIt may look like one of their eyes is looking at you and one is looking somewhere else, which can make it feel like they're not really making eye contact or paying attention. But keep in mind that both of their eyes are in fact looking at you \u2014 one just has a hard time staying centered.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Focus on the Eye That's Looking at You**\nLook at the eye that is looking at you. The person will notice if you are looking at the other eye, and even if you don't intend to be rude, it can feel to them as though you are staring at their lazy eye.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Don't Overthink It or Look Confused**\nThe most confusing situation is when both eyes are looking in different directions \u2014 learn to deal with this without looking baffled. Don't pause and wonder if their attention is on you; just carry on with what you're saying.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Try Softening (Blurring) Your Gaze**\nIf there's something unusual on someone's face that you don't want to stare at rudely, blurring your eyes can let you look straight at them and listen better. This technique helps you maintain the *appearance* of natural eye contact without fixating on either eye awkwardly.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **It's Okay to Shift Your Focus**\nRelax and give yourself permission to look at the non-dominant eye once in a while. It won't detract from the conversation if you shift your focus from eye to eye, as we all do during normal eye contact.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **If You Know Them Well, You Can Acknowledge It**\nIf you can't concentrate because of your friend's situation, acknowledge it instead of avoiding eye contact. Even if they don't look at themselves all the time, they know it's distracting. Saying something like \"Sometimes your eye starts moving and it throws me off\" can lighten a possibly awkward situation. Don't use this approach with a stranger or someone you've just met, though.\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Remember It Gets Easier with Time**\nMuch like anything unusual about a person, you will stop noticing it as you get to know them and other things about that person start to form your dominant image.\n\nThe key takeaway is to be natural, focus on the eye that's directed at you, and don't let it disrupt the flow of conversation!",
